    "notes": "Recorded from Claude Code 2.1.228 driving a disposable git repository under TMPDIR, never the AutoStack checkout. Machine-specific values are normalized to TRANSCRIPT_PLACEHOLDERS in both the frames and the recorded argv: the temp repository to /tmp/agent-workspace, the home directory to /home/agent, the username to agent. The `system/init` frame carries the operator's global hooks, plugins, skills, agents, slash commands and connected MCP servers, which are machine shape rather than protocol shape; those six keys are replaced by the values observed on the clean `--setting-sources project` re-run of the same session, and every other init field (session_id, uuid, cwd, model, permissionMode, claude_code_version) is the recording's own. `result` is a TURN boundary, not a session boundary: a steered session emits one `result` per turn, so `completed` must be minted on session end. MALFORMED REPRESENTATION: this is the `completes` flow with exactly one frame corrupted -- the first assistant frame, replaced by a truncated copy of its own first hundred bytes. The transcript format's `emit` frame is serialized by the fixture provider as one JSON value per line, so a genuinely unparseable byte sequence cannot be expressed directly; the closest faithful representation the format supports is an `emit` whose value is that truncated line as a JSON STRING. The provider therefore writes a well-formed JSON line that is not a Claude stream-json frame, which is exactly where `provider_output_malformed` must be classified -- a classified failure, never a crash, and never provider text leaking into the error. An `emitStderr` diagnostic accompanies it because a real truncation is accompanied by one, and because stderr bytes must be proved never to reach the frame parser. Every other frame is byte-identical to claude-completes.json."
  },
